\section{Introduction}
Western Neo-Aramaic (ISO 639-3 amw) is spoken in Ma'lula and Jubb'adin in Syria (Bakh'a is reported depopulated). Sources disagree on speaker numbers. This report lists what appeared in search results.

\section{Primary Educational Resources}
Search results mention Anas Abou-Ismail, \emph{Western Neo-Aramaic: The Dialect of Jubaadin} (Cambridge Scholars Publishing, 2020), an English textbook; no URL for it was seen. No school programme was found.

\section{Linguistic and Reference Materials}
\begin{itemize}
\item Endangered Languages Project profile with bibliography: \url{https://endangeredlanguages.com/node/39045}
\item Omniglot page on Western Neo-Aramaic: \url{https://omniglot.com/writing/westernneoaramaic.htm}
\item Wikipedia, Maaloula: \url{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maaloula}
\end{itemize}
Werner's \emph{Das Neuwestaram\"aische} (1990) is cited as the standard grammar (no URL seen).

\section{Religious and Community Resources}
\begin{itemize}
\item Scripture Earth, Bible portions with Global Recordings audio: \url{https://scriptureearth.org/00eng.php?iso=amw}
\end{itemize}

\section{Digital and Audio Resources}
\begin{itemize}
\item Archive of written records of Western Neo-Aramaic (Aymenn Jawad): \url{https://aymennjawad.org/24069/archive-of-written-records-of-western-neo-aramaic}
\end{itemize}

\section{Curriculum Development Context}
Printed scholarship exists, but community-facing teaching materials are not evident. Conflict has displaced speakers, so any curriculum work needs speaker partners.
\end{document}
